A. What is scikit-learn?

scikit-learn is one of the most widely used ML libraries in Python. It provides simple, consistent APIs to build, train, evaluate, and deploy classical ML models. 
This library provides:
1. Ready-Made ML algorithms - Train ML models with few lines of code 
    Linear Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, SVM, K-Means, PCA, Naive Bayes, KNN 
2. Tools to prepare and clean data 
    Train/test split, Scaling, Encoding categorical variables, Imputation, Pipelines to combine all preprocessing + model in clean workflow 
3. Evaluation tools 
    Perfect for comparing models - accuracy, precision, recall, ROC, AUC, confusion matrix, cross validation, grid search and random search
4. Works best for tabular/structured data 
    Student score prediction, Salary prediction, Fraud detection, Customer Churn, House Price prediction, credit risk scoring 

scikit-learn does not handle Deep Learning (use PyTorch or TensorFlow), GPUs, Very large datasets beyond RAM, Unstructured data like images/audio/text (needs DL) 

B. How scikit works internally?

1. scikit-learn uses the "Estimator API Pattern". Every model or transformer in scikit-learn follows exactly the same design pattern:
    The 3 step pattern:
        1. create an object (estimator)
        2. Fit it [learn from data]
        3. use it 

2. Internally, a model is a python class with parameters + learned attributes 
    Before training it has hyperparameters
    After training it stores learned parameters
    hyperparameters = tuning knobs
    parameters = learned from data

3. fit() implements the core math of each algorithm 
    Algorithms are Linear Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, SVM

4. Predict / Transform use the learned parameters 


5. Pipelines glue preprocessing + model into one object
    

6. Cross Validation is automated loops 

7. GridSearchCV / RandomizedSearchCV clone models and run many experiments

8. scikit-learn is optimized but NOT GPU based
    Internally it uses NumPy (vectorized operations), SciPy (linear algebra), and Cython (C-speed sections). Fast for small/medium structured datasets, not for DL 

9. Memory-based design 
    scikit-learn expects - data fit in RAM, X is a 2D array, and y is a 1D array. This keeps the library clean and simple at the cost of scale. 

10. Internal design philosphy 
    1. Consistency - Everything uses .fit(), .predict(), .transform()
    2. Composition - mix preprocessing + model using pipelines
    3. Stateless API - Transformers and models store internal states only after fit

Internally, Scikit-learn is a collection of machine learning algorithms wrapped in a uniform Estimator API, built on top of NumPy/SciPy, 
with consistent fit/transform/predict behavior, pipelines for composition, and efficient implementations of classical ML math.


